## Phosphinic Acid Based Linkers: New Building Block in MOF Chemistry
Metal-organic frameworks are coordination polymers with an open framework containing potential voids. Their structures are composed of inorganic nodes connected by organic linkers.[1,2] The chemical and hydrothermal stability of MOFs is crucial for most potential applications including gas storage and separation,[3] catalysis,[4] sensing,[5] and medicinal applications.[6] Since the discovery of the first MOF structures, carboxylatebased MOFs have been in the spotlight.[1,2] Unfortunately, the majority of carboxylate-based MOFs display low stability in aqueous media. The exceptions are MOFs consisting of tetravalent cations, e.g., the Zr(IV)-based UiO-66 family,[7,8] and some trivalent cations, e.g., Fe(III), Al(III), and Cr(III) based MIL53 and MIL-101.[9-11] However, even from these mentioned MOFs only a few can withstand elevated temperatures in water without significant loss of porosity.[12]
One possible strategy for the preparation of water-stable MOFs is to use linker groups that form very strong bonds with tri- and tetra-valent cations. In this context, linkers based on diaryl or dialkylphosphinates (with the general formula ${\mathsf{R}}_{2}{\mathsf{P O O H}})$ show promise as they can form coordination architectures similar to those of carboxylates and yet do so, in accordance to the Hard and Soft Acids and Bases (HSAB) Theory, with stronger bonds to hard metals like Fe(III) and $Z r(|\vee)$ . Perhaps then surprisingly phosphinates have obtained much less attention for the preparation of coordination polymers[13] especially in comparison with carboxylic acid based linkers.[2] Until now only monophosphinic acids,[14-16] bisphosphinic acids with a short spacer,[17,18] or bisphosphinic acids with a flexible spacer[19] have been used for the preparation of coordination polymers (see Fig.
1). None of these linkers, however, produced coordination polymers displaying permanent porosity.
Nevertheless, encouraged by the coordination potential of phosphinic acid and the chemical versatility of its phosphorus centres (as opposed to the limitations of the carboxylic acid group), we endeavored to probe further its possibilities as a linking group. For this reason we synthetized an analogue of terephtalic acid, the most common carboxylate linker used in MOFs – phenylene-1,4-bis(methylphosphinic acid) (PBPA) (see Fig. 1). We subsequently discovered that under solvothermal conditions PBPA forms with $F e C l_{3}$ three new structures that we have denoted ICR-1, ICR-2, and ICR-3 (where ICR stands for Institute of Inorganic Chemistry Řež), of which ICR-2 is a porous MOF with hydrothermal stability superior to that of its MIL-53 cousin.
Initially, the nanocrystalline powders obtained of ICR-1, -2, and - 3 were characterized by powder XRD, however, the crystal size and crystallinity did not permit full structure elucidation. To remedy this, we employed electron diffraction tomography (EDT), which over the last decade has evolved into a powerful tool for structure determination of microcrystalline powders.[20,21] The structure solution of MOFs by EDT data analysis is often highly challenging due to poor sample crystallinity, and/or fast degradation of crystals in the electron beam. Due to these reasons, no MOF structure so far has been completely solved ab-initio from EDT, i.e., solved purely based on the EDT without prior assumptions about the structure.
UiO-66 was solved by a combination of electron imaging (highresolution transmission electron microscopy) and EDT[22] while the structure of ZIF-8 was determined solely by electron imaging.[23] The structure of CAU-7 was solved from EDT data, but simulated annealing had to be used for structure solution.[24] Recently, MOFs containing $\mathsf{M}_{12}(\upmu_{3}{-}\mathsf{O})_{8}(\upmu^{3}{-}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H})_{8}(\upmu_{2}{-}\mathsf{O}\mathsf{H})_{6}$ $(\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{Z r}$ , Hf) secondary building units were solved solely from EDT data, however, only $Z\boldsymbol{\mathsf{r}}$ or Hf atoms were solved ab-initio and the rest of the structure was completed based on the difference potential maps and a prior knowledge of the organic linkers.[25] The only structure where eight out of nine non-hydrogen atoms were solved ab-initio is MFU-4l.[26]
In this work, we employed full dynamical diffraction theory in the least-squares refinement (dynamical refinement) of the EDT data instead of the commonly used kinematical approximation. This advanced technique allowed a clear localization of all hydrogen atoms in the two non-porous coordination structures ICR-1 and ICR-3 and localization of seven out of fifteen hydrogen atoms in the MOF structure ICR-2.
All compounds were prepared by solvothermal crystallization of phenylene-1,4-bis(methylphosphinic acid) (PBPA) with $F e C l_{3}.6H_{2}O$ in a PTFE-lined stainless steel autoclave. ICR-1 is formed in water with the presence of HF as a modulator at $220^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . ICR-2 can be prepared in various organic solvents (e.g., acetone, acetonitrile, and toluene), however, the best crystallinity was obtained in EtOH at $250^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . Both ICR-1 and ICR-2 were prepared using a PBPA:Fe molar ratio of 2:1, whereas ICR-3 was prepared with a PBPA:Fe molar ratio of 1:2 in DMF at $220^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ .
The structures of the MOF ICR-2 and the two coordination polymers ICR-1 and ICR-3 were solved ab-initio; the structure models contained 9, 22, and 15 independent non-hydrogen atoms for ICR-1, ICR-2, and ICR-3, respectively. For the first time, the dynamical refinement[27-30] of a MOF structure was used and to good success, clearly locating all hydrogen atoms in the structures of ICR-1 and ICR-3.[31] The fibre-like nanocrystals of ICR-2 were significantly twisted, which in combination with rather low stability under the electron beam (apparent loss of crystallinity occurred after a dose of about $5~{\tt e}^{\cdot}\mathbb{A}^{-2}.$ ) restricted the available dataset (only about one quarter of the frames were available in comparison with the other two structures). Despite these handicaps, seven out of fifteen hydrogen atoms were located, albeit at the noise level. For more detailed information concerning synthesis and structural characterisation, see the ESI.
ICR-1 and ICR-2 form 3D structures consisting of 1D infinity chains of octahedrally coordinated iron atoms bound together through O-P-O bridges. The phenylene bridge connects these columns forming two different 3D systems, see Fig. 2, S3 and S4 in the ESI. On the other hand, ICR-3 is a layered structure consisting of bilayers held together by hydrophobic/van der Waals interactions. Interestingly, the structure of ICR-1 is isoreticular with Zr bisphosphonate prepared by M. Taddei et al.[32]
While ICR-1 and ICR-3 represent dense phases bereft of any void space, ICR-2 is comprised of a honeycomb pattern of micropores running parallel to the one-dimensional chains of iron atoms (see Fig. 2 bottom diagram). The largest sphere that can freely fit into the channels (pore limiting diameter, PLD) is 9 Å in diameter as calculated by the program Poreblazer.[33] These channels are isolated and do not permit gas crossing even upon dynamic rotation of the linker groups’ phenyl rings (See Figure S3 and accompanying text in the ESI an elaboration on this statement).
Because of the similarity of PBPA with terephtalic acid, one might expect analogous MOFs to be formed. Indeed, the structural motif of infinite chains of octahedrally coordinated iron atoms is present in both structures; see Fig. 3 and S5 in the ESI. However, the difference in the structure of the linking groups leads to quite different crystal packing. Thus, in MIL-53 the iron atoms are octahedrally coordinated by four oxygen atoms, coming from terephtalic acid groups, and by a further two oxygen atoms, originating from bridging hydroxyl groups, resulting in the formation of infinite chains bound together in such a fashion as to generate a wine rack array of 1D channels. In the case of ICR-2, however, there are no bridging hydroxyl groups in the infinite chain of octahedrally coordinated iron atoms, and as a consequence the 1D channels are arranged into a honeycomb array (see Fig S5 in the ESI).
ICR-3 is also comprised of 1D infinity chains of iron atoms coordinated by O-P-O bridges; however, the building system significantly differs from ICR-1 or ICR-2. The oxygen atoms around the iron atoms are coordinated in a trigonal bipyramid formation and every two neighbouring bipyramids are edgesharing. In the chain, pairs of edge-shared bipyramids are connected through vertices by four tetrahedrons of phosphinic acid, see Fig. S3, in the ESI. The 1D chains form 2D bilayers held together only by weak non-bonding interactions, see Fig. 2. The permanent porosity of ICR-2 was probed by ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption. The adsorption isotherm, which may be seen in Fig. S18 in the ESI, displays a type I shape, which is typical for microporous solids. The surface area of $H_{2}O$ treated ICR-2 of $819\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (see Table 1, Entry 2) is in a good agreement with the theoretical value of $850~\mathsf{m}^{2}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ calculated from the crystal structure.[34,35] Also the calculated pore diameter of $8\mathsf{A}$ using the micropore analysis (MP) method is in good agreement with a PLD value of 9 Å determined from the crystal structure.
Structural stability and integrity upon solvothermal treatment is a critical parameter for the application of MOFs. No degradation of ICR-2 was observed after treatment at RT in common solvents, e.g., acetone, EtOH, or $H_{2}O$ . To find the limits, we also investigated the stability of ICR-2 in boiling toluene, water, and ethanol. In Table 1 are summarized the surface areas and pore diameters of solvothermally treated ICR-2 (for adsorption isotherms see Fig. S20 and S21 in the ESI). It is clear that in a non-coordinating solvent like toluene the structure remains intact even after $24\ h$ at reflux temperature. In boiling water the structure is highly stable, retaining $78\%$ of the original surface area even after $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . For comparison, the analogous $\mathsf{F e}$ -based MOF MIL-53 recrystallizes to a non-porous phase under reflux in water. The hydrothermal stability of ICR-2 can be compared to $Z\mathsf{r}(\mathsf{I V})$ -based terephthalate MOF UiO-66 which loses $12\%$ of its surface area after $5\mathfrak{h}$ exposure to steam.[12] The lowest stability for ICR-2 was observed in boiling ethanol where the surface area decreases to $36\%$ of the original value. This decrease in porosity was not due to dissolution of ICR-2 as evidenced by the absence of PBPA in the EtOH solution (analysed by HPLC see ESI).
Degradation mechanisms for MOFs are generally poorly understood, and indeed rationalization of the lower stability of ICR-2 in ethanol is unclear. Nevertheless, in an effort to gain some insight into the possible degradation mechanisms, (TGA) thermogravimetric analysis (see Fig. S22-24 in the ESI) was used to investigate the thermal decomposition of the new materials. In air, ICR-1 and ICR-2 begin decomposing at ${\sim}450~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ whereas ICR-3 is stable up to approximately $500^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . In all cases the decomposition is accompanied by the evolution of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and $H_{2}O$ along with smaller amounts of acetone and ${\sf H}_{3}{\sf P}{\sf O}_{4}$ . Under Ar, ICR-2 was stable to approximately $500^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Fig. S25 in the ESI). In this case, acetone and ${\sf H}_{3}{\sf P}{\sf O}_{4}$ were the most prevalent evolved volatiles. In comparison with similar MOF structures based on carboxylic acids (UiO-66, MIL-53, and MIL101) ICR-2 does not display significant weight loss corresponding to the thermal release of adsorbed water molecules. This is probably due to the absence of -OH groups in the structure of ICR-2 which makes the pores less hydrophilic. It has not escaped our attention that the lower hydrophilicity of our ICR-2 system could be beneficial in terms of the time and energy saved in not having to dry the system before adsorption, or the possibility to use ICR-2 in applications requiring anhydrous conditions.
Regarding the perspectives of our new system, of additional importance is the chemical versatility of the phosphinic acid. For comparison, carboxylic acid based linkers can only be modified by changing the spacer group between the carboxylic acids, whereas phosphinic acids can be additionally modified by alkyl or aryl groups at the phosphorus atom, see Fig. 4. In the case of ICR-2 the methyl group of the phosphinic acid points inwards along the length of the pores and therefore substitution with other groups would lead to modified pore size and/or pores with additional functionality. One could conceive of porous channels in the ICR-2 system tailored to selectively favour interactions with specific fluids/molecules based on the appropriate selection of the “P-OPTIONAL” group as shown in Figure 4.
To prove the validity of this concept, we prepared a second MOF from this new series (ICR-4) using phenylene-1,4- bis(phenylphosphinic acid) as the linking group that features phenyl rings in the place of methyl groups present in ICR-2. Its structure is isoreticular with ICR-2, but with pores lined with phenyl rings (see Fig. 4) and a PLD of $3\mathsf{A}$ . These pores are still accessible to ${\sf N}_{2}$ as evidenced in the adsorption isotherm of ICR4 shown in Fig. S31 in the ESI, however, the surface area decreases to $165\:\mathsf{m}^{2}\:\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . For experimental details see ESI.
